{% research-status value="Proposed" /%}

## Experiment ID and title

**EXP-001 — HTML Structure**

## Objective and research question

Compare whether representation and semantic structure of equivalent content affects content extraction, structural interpretation, retrieval, or answer outcomes. Addresses RQ1 and supports H-001 and H-002.

## Related hypothesis

- H-001 — Semantic HTML vs. generic DOM
- H-002 — DOM hierarchy and content extraction

## Background

Semantic markup and section hierarchy may expose structure, but the evaluated parsers and retrieval systems may ignore, transform, or use those signals. This design tests variants; it does not presume an effect.

## Independent variables

- **Primary factor:** generic container elements vs. corresponding semantic HTML5 elements, while matching text, nesting depth, order, visibility, and page delivery.
- **Separate exploratory arms:** plain text and semantic HTML5 with ARIA where native semantics are insufficient. Do not combine these arms into the primary estimate: transport and ARIA introduce additional factors.
- DOM hierarchy or complexity is a separate factor and requires its own contrast.

## Dependent variables

Predefine measures for target content extraction, section/heading association, retrieval relevance, answer quality, and citation behavior. See [metrics](/en/05-experiments/metrics/).

## Control variables

Exact content and fact set, wording, URLs/hosting where feasible, styling and visibility, metadata, query set, parser/retriever/model configuration, timing, and run count. Record unavoidable differences, especially the plain-text delivery path.

## Experimental setup and test variants

Prepare matched fixture pairs for the primary comparison: (A) generic containers and (B) semantically corresponding HTML5 elements with otherwise matched tree shape. Maintain a content manifest and verify text/fact parity, order, visibility, and HTTP delivery. Optional (C) ARIA and (D) plain-text arms are reported separately as exploratory comparisons. Record any semantic role that cannot be matched structurally as a design difference.

## Test procedure

1. Freeze the fixture pair, content manifest, test set, primary outcome, and analysis plan.
2. Verify response and rendered content, tree shape, and content parity for every fixture.
3. Randomize fixture/run order within content blocks; execute the generic-versus-semantic contrast in the same declared pipeline and time window.
4. Score extraction and section association against a frozen reference; blind annotators to the variant when the output allows it.
5. Treat plain text, ARIA, retrieval, and generated answers as distinct exploratory or secondary arms; do not infer downstream benefits from extraction alone.
6. Preserve failures and report results per content item, pipeline stage, and repetition.

## Tools and metrics

**Tools:** Not selected. Document names and versions before execution.

**Primary candidate:** field-level extraction F1 or exact fact recovery against a frozen gold set, selected after a pilot and frozen before confirmation. **Secondary:** section association accuracy. Retrieval and answer/citation metrics require a separately defined pipeline and analysis.

## Results and interpretation

Not run. No results or interpretation recorded.

## Limitations

Plain text may differ in transport and available metadata. ARIA can add signals or introduce noise; it is not appropriate as a blanket addition. Semantic element substitution may also alter browser accessibility semantics, which should be measured or checked as a guardrail. Results are specific to selected content, parsers, and downstream systems.

## Reproducibility information

Not yet specified. Dataset, fixture source, tool versions, run environment, protocol revision, run order, and raw-output archive must be recorded before execution. Use the shared [analysis plan](/en/05-experiments/analysis-plan/).

